{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And I will make of you a great nation, etc.</b> He showed him that even those three imagined benefits that you have from your land and your birthplace and your father's house — you will have them there as well. For if you go from your birthplace, the place of your family, and you feel that in time of need you will have neither brothers nor those who show you mercy — regarding this He said, 'And I will make of you a great nation,' for from your loins there too many families will come forth. And if you are concerned about the inheritance of wealth — behold, here too 'I will bless you,' also in wealth. And if you are concerned about the good name that you have in your land, a place where people know you — 'and I will make your name great,' here too. And corresponding to the statement 'go for yourself' — that is, for your own self — He said, 'and you shall be a blessing': you will be the source of blessings through your cleaving to that holy place from which the flow goes forth to all the world, and it is the source of blessings, for the waters of blessing go forth from the Temple, and through the demand of the holy land the Holy One, blessed be He, demands all the other lands. It follows that through your cleaving to the place that is the source of blessings, you too will be a source for all blessings, and through your cleaving there to the Shekhinah your mouth will be like the mouth of HaShem, blessed be He, and it will be in your hand to bless whomever you wish." "And in tractate Pesachim (117b) [it is taught]: 'And I will make of you a great nation' — this is why they say 'Elohei Avraham' etc., for Abraham became a great nation more than all the patriarchs, for all that issued from Isaac and Jacob is traced also to Abraham, in addition to Isaac and Jacob, for from him issued Ishmael and the sons of Keturah. 'And I will bless you' — in wealth; this is why they say 'Elohei Yitzchak,' for he was blessed with wealth more than all of them, as it says (Genesis 26:12), 'And Isaac sowed in that land, and found in that year a hundredfold, and YHWH blessed him.' 'And I will make your name great' — this is why they say 'Elohei Ya'akov,' as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Megillah 18a), 'And he called it El' (Genesis 33:20) — the Holy One, blessed be He, called Jacob 'El' etc. And according to the plain sense he was called by the name Yisrael on account of 'for you have striven with Elohim and with men' (Genesis 32:29), but Abraham was nothing other than 'father of a multitude of nations' (Genesis 17:5)."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {1 "Citation of Pesachim corrected to 117b (folio number קיז: read as 117, not 113); the passage corresponds to b. Pesachim 117a-b in standard pagination."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:02.822128735Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}